= Data structure — Technical =

== Representation and operations ==
A data structure can be modeled as a representation space together with operations constrained by invariants. The performance and correctness of an operation are properties of the combination of:

* representation;
* operation implementation;
* input distribution;
* computational/storage model;
* maintained invariants.

This is why a data structure should not be assigned a context-free label such as simply "fast."

== Abstract data types ==
An [[Abstract data type|ADT]] specifies values and observable operations without committing to an internal representation. Liskov and Zilles's work on abstract data types helped establish this separation as a major software-abstraction principle.

A concrete data structure can implement an ADT, while several data structures can implement the same ADT with different cost and persistence properties.

== Representation invariants ==
A representation invariant defines conditions that valid concrete states must satisfy.

Examples include:
* ordering constraints in a binary-search tree;
* heap-order properties;
* structural balance conditions;
* hash-table occupancy/collision invariants;
* graph-representation consistency.

Hoare's work on correctness of data representations formalized how abstraction functions and representation invariants can be used to reason about implementations.

== Cost models ==
Standard operation bounds may be:
* worst-case;
* expected;
* average-case;
* amortized.

More realistic systems may require I/O, cache, communication, synchronization, persistence, or energy costs.

External-memory structures minimize block transfers. Concurrent structures must account for interleavings and synchronization. Distributed structures add communication and failure assumptions.

== Persistence and mutability ==
A mutable structure changes an existing representation. A persistent structure preserves access to previous versions after updates.

Functional programming often uses persistent immutable structures, while imperative systems commonly update structures in place. Neither choice is universally superior; performance, sharing, concurrency, and reasoning requirements determine the trade-off.

== Examples of representation trade-offs ==
An adjacency matrix offers constant-time edge-presence checks but uses quadratic space in the number of vertices. An adjacency-list representation is usually more compact for sparse graphs and supports efficient neighbor iteration.

A hash table typically gives efficient expected lookup but depends on hashing assumptions and collision handling. Balanced search trees give ordered traversal and logarithmic worst-case operations under maintained balance invariants.

